==Finnish==

===Etymology===
Probably from {{der|fi|gmq-osw|siel}}, {{m|gmq-osw|siäl||soul}}, compare modern {{cog|sv|själ}}, {{cog|de|Seele}} and {{cog|en|soul}}.
Alternatively, borrowed from {{der|fi|bat}} *sḗjlā (compare {{cog|lt|siela}}), along with other Finnish loanwords from Baltic languages of the same thematic group, see etymology sections of e.g.taivas (“heaven”), Jumala (“God”).

